%% CASE Meridian Bank core banking modernisation
Meridian Bank is replacing its core banking platform. Four agile teams build and integrate the software components; data migration and the rollout to 120 branches follow a predictive plan.

- The financial regulator requires **notification 60 days before** any change to a critical system and **evidence of tested recovery procedures**.
- The cut-over weekend is the riskiest event: if migration fails, branches could not open on Monday.
- **1,200 branch employees** will use new processes; many fear the change will mean more work and fewer jobs.
- A fintech competitor has just launched **instant loans**, and **interest rates are rising** quickly.
@@ BE2 | Hybrid
How should the project handle the regulator's requirements?
A) Schedule the notification 60 days ahead and require tested recovery evidence
B) Notify the regulator after the cut-over, once the new platform is proven to be working
C) Ask the vendor to deal with the regulator, since the platform software is the vendor's product
D) Treat the recovery tests as optional, since the agile teams test the software continuously
= A
? Regulatory obligations must be built into the plan as mandatory milestones and deliverables. Notifying afterwards or treating evidence as optional breaches compliance.

@@ BE5 | Hybrid
What is the best response to the risk of a failed cut-over?
A) Rehearse the migration, define go/no-go criteria and prepare a tested rollback plan
B) Accept the risk, since the agile teams have already tested every software component
C) Transfer the risk to the branches by asking them to prepare manual procedures alone
D) Avoid the risk by postponing the cut-over until all possible risks have disappeared
= A
? Rehearsals reduce probability, go/no-go criteria control the decision, and a rollback (fallback) plan limits impact. Indefinite postponement is not realistic.

@@ BE7 | Hybrid
How should the project support the branch employees through the change?
A) Use a structured change approach: explain why, involve champions, train, reinforce
B) Announce the new processes on go-live day so that employees have less time to worry about them
C) Tell employees that the new processes will not affect their workload or their jobs in any way
D) Leave the change to branch managers, since the project's responsibility ends with the software
= A
? A structured change approach (for example, ADKAR) builds awareness, desire, knowledge, ability and reinforcement. False assurances and late announcements increase resistance.

@@ BE8 | Hybrid
How should the project respond to the competitor's launch and rising interest rates?
A) Assess the business case impact with the sponsor; product owners reprioritise
B) Ignore both developments, since the platform's scope was approved before they happened
C) Add instant loans to the scope immediately so that the bank can match the competitor
D) Stop the project until interest rates stabilise and the market becomes clearer
= A
? External changes can alter value and priorities. They are analysed with decision-makers, and backlog priorities are adjusted through the product owners.
%% END CASE

@@ BE1 | Predictive
A publicly funded transit project is overseen by a citizens' board. The board asks how major decisions are made and recorded. What should the governance framework provide?
A) Clear decision rights, documented decisions and transparent reporting to the board
B) Decisions made informally by the project manager to save time and avoid bureaucracy
C) Confidential decision-making so that the board does not interfere with the project
D) Decisions made by the main contractor, who carries most of the delivery risk
= A
? Public projects need transparent governance: who decides, how decisions are recorded and how oversight bodies are informed.

@@ BE1 | Agile
The steering committee wants to approve every change to the order of the product backlog. What is the most appropriate governance arrangement?
A) The product owner orders it within guardrails; the committee reviews outcomes
B) The committee approves every backlog change, since it is ultimately accountable for the product
C) The developers order the backlog, and the committee approves their decisions at each review
D) The project manager orders the backlog, and the product owner advises the committee on it
= A
? Effective agile governance delegates backlog decisions to the product owner within agreed boundaries, while governance bodies focus on outcomes and direction.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid
Two companies form a joint venture to deliver a project. Each wants its own processes to prevail. What should be established first?
A) A joint governance structure with agreed decision rights
B) The processes of the company with the larger share of the joint venture
C) Separate governance for each company's own part of the project
D) Governance only once the first serious disagreement has occurred
= A
? Joint ventures need shared governance agreed upfront to make decisions and resolve disputes effectively.

@@ BE1 | Predictive
Halfway through the project, the sponsor is replaced. What should the project manager do?
A) Review the charter, business case and status with the new sponsor
B) Continue as before, since the charter remains valid regardless of who the sponsor is
C) Pause the project until the new sponsor has had time to issue a new charter
D) Ask the previous sponsor to keep approving decisions until the project ends
= A
? A new sponsor may have different expectations; confirming the charter, business case and direction maintains alignment and authority.

@@ BE1 | Agile
An organisation manages a portfolio of agile products. How can portfolio governance give teams autonomy while keeping investments aligned with strategy?
A) Set strategic themes and budget guardrails, and review outcomes
B) Approve each product team's sprint backlog before every sprint starts
C) Fund products once for several years without any further reviews
D) Require all products to follow the same detailed roadmap set centrally
= A
? Lean portfolio governance uses themes, guardrails and regular outcome reviews instead of approving detailed work.

@@ BE1 | Hybrid | multi
The organisation is introducing AI tools into project work. Which two governance elements are most important? (Choose two.)
A) A list of approved tools and rules for the data that may be used with them
B) Requirements for human review of AI outputs before important decisions
C) A rule that AI outputs never need to be checked by people
D) Permission for each team member to use any tool they prefer
E) A ban on discussing AI use with stakeholders
= A, B
? Responsible AI governance covers approved tools, data protection and human accountability for decisions.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
During an aircraft modification, the engineers want to change a certified design to save weight. What must happen before the change is implemented?
A) It must be approved by the aviation authority under the certification process
B) The chief engineer can approve it, since weight savings improve the aircraft's performance
C) The change can be made and reported to the authority at the next scheduled inspection
D) The change can be made if the customer agrees to accept responsibility for it
= A
? Changes to certified designs require approval from the certification authority; internal or customer approval is not sufficient.

@@ BE2 | Agile
Banking rules require that every change to the payment software is approved by a second person before release. How can the agile team comply efficiently?
A) Build the second approval into its workflow, such as mandatory peer review in the pipeline, with an audit trail
B) Ask the compliance department to review every change manually at the end of each quarter
C) Release the changes first and collect the second approvals in a monthly batch afterwards
D) Ask the regulator for an exemption, since agile teams release changes too often for the rule
= A
? Controls such as the "four-eyes" principle can be built into the delivery workflow, which keeps the team compliant without slowing it down.

@@ BE2 | Predictive
A stadium concert must comply with crowd safety regulations, which require an approved safety plan and inspections before tickets go on sale. Ticket sales are scheduled to start next week, but the plan is not yet approved. What should the project manager do?
A) Delay ticket sales until the safety plan is approved
B) Start ticket sales and finish the safety plan afterwards
C) Sell tickets only to members of the fan club first
D) Ask the venue to take responsibility for the plan
= A
? Regulatory prerequisites must be met before the activities they govern; safety requirements cannot be bypassed.

@@ BE2 | Hybrid
A new account-opening process must meet anti-money-laundering requirements. What should the project manager do?
A) Have compliance define the controls and build them into requirements and tests
B) Implement the process first and ask the compliance team to review it after the launch date
C) Ask the developers to research the anti-money-laundering rules on their own
D) Remove the controls from the first release to simplify the customer experience
= A
? Mandatory controls are defined with compliance experts and built into requirements and tests from the start.

@@ BE2 | Agile
A national law requires that customers' personal data be stored only within the country. The team wants to use a cloud service hosted abroad. What should happen?
A) Verify the residency rules and choose a compliant hosting option
B) Use the service, since cloud providers are responsible for legal compliance
C) Encrypt the data and store it abroad without checking the law
D) Use the service for now and move the data later if anyone complains
= A
? Legal data residency requirements must be met; the team selects compliant options after verifying the rules.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
Who should typically sit on a change control board for a large construction project?
A) People with authority over scope, cost and technical decisions, per governance
B) Only the project manager, who knows the project better than any other stakeholder
C) The newest team members, so that they can learn about the project's decisions
D) Anyone who has submitted a change request, so that they can approve their own changes
= A
? CCB membership is defined by governance and includes people with appropriate authority and expertise.

@@ BE3 | Agile
Business stakeholders want to change priorities every week, which disrupts the team mid-sprint. What is the best way to handle this?
A) Channel changes through the product owner so they enter at sprint boundaries
B) Allow stakeholders to change sprint work whenever their priorities change
C) Freeze all priorities for the whole release so that nothing disrupts the team
D) Ask the sponsor to forbid stakeholders from changing their priorities at all
= A
? Agile embraces change, but through the backlog and sprint cadence, protecting each sprint's focus.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
A change made by an agile team to the customer app means that branch staff's training materials, which a predictive workstream produces, must also change. How should this be handled?
A) Raise it through the integrated change process, so that the training workstream can plan the update
B) Ask the agile team to update the training materials itself, without telling the other workstream
C) Leave the training materials unchanged, since branch staff will discover the change themselves
D) Reverse the change to the app, since it affects another workstream's approved deliverables
= A
? In hybrid projects, a change in one workstream can affect another. Integrated change control makes the impact visible so that all affected work is updated.

@@ BE3 | Predictive
Thirty change requests are waiting for evaluation, and the change control board meets monthly. Project work is slowing down. What should the project manager do?
A) Prioritise them by urgency and impact, and propose faster or delegated decisions
B) Approve all thirty requests at once so that the backlog is cleared quickly
C) Reject all of the requests that have been waiting for more than two weeks
D) Ask the team to implement the requests while waiting for approval
= A
? Change control should be effective and proportionate; prioritising and adjusting the decision cadence or delegation addresses the bottleneck.

@@ BE3 | Agile
Two weeks before a release, the product owner wants to add a feature, but this would put the release date at risk. What should happen?
A) The product owner weighs its value against the risk and may trade scope
B) The team adds the feature and works overtime to keep the release date
C) The scrum master refuses the request, since the release scope is now fixed
D) The release is postponed automatically so that the feature can be included
= A
? The product owner makes value trade-offs, informed by the team, to protect the release goal.

@@ BE3 | Hybrid
On a construction site, the client's representative verbally instructs the contractor's foreman to move a wall. What should the project manager do?
A) Document it as a change request and process it through change control
B) Let the foreman move the wall, since the client has requested it
C) Ignore the instruction completely, since it was not given in writing
D) Ask the client's representative to arrange the wall move personally
= A
? Verbal instructions must be captured formally so the change can be assessed and approved before work is done.

@@ BE4 | Agile
Stories are often blocked because the compliance department reviews them only at the end of each sprint. What should the scrum master do?
A) Have a compliance representative join backlog refinement
B) Ask the team to skip compliance reviews for minor stories to save time
C) Move all of the compliance reviews to the end of the release instead
D) Accept the blocks as a normal part of working in a regulated bank
= A
? Bringing compliance into refinement surfaces requirements early and removes a recurring impediment.

@@ BE4 | Predictive
A permit from the city is two months late, delaying construction. The project team has followed up several times without success. What should the project manager do?
A) Escalate through the sponsor or agreed channels to engage the city
B) Start construction without the permit to avoid further delays
C) Wait for the permit without taking any further action on the issue
